steps.body:
Two nodes are in the same **connected component** when some path joins them. To collect one component, start a DFS at a node and visit everything it can reach. It is the island count from the recursion lesson, with adjacency lists in place of grid neighbors. Recursion works too, but a long path needs one nested call per node, and Go stops the program when a goroutine's stack grows past 1 GB. An **iterative DFS** keeps its own stack in a slice instead:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	adj := [][]int{{1}, {0, 2}, {1}, {}}
	visited := make([]bool, len(adj))
	start := 0
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(visited)
}
```

```go
stack := []int{start}
visited[start] = true
for len(stack) > 0 {
	u := stack[len(stack)-1]
	stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]
	for _, v := range adj[u] {
		if !visited[v] {
			visited[v] = true
			stack = append(stack, v)
		}
	}
}
```

- `visited := make([]bool, n)` remembers seen nodes. Mark a node when you **push** it, or a node reachable from two neighbors gets pushed twice. The visit order then differs a little from recursive DFS, which does not matter for components.
- To **count components**, loop over every node and start a new DFS at each unvisited one. Every start is one component.
- To **measure a component**, count the nodes you pop.

Pass `visited` to a helper function freely. Only the slice header is copied, and it still points at the same booleans, so marks made inside the helper are visible to the caller.

example.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

func component(adj [][]int, visited []bool, start int) []int {
	stack := []int{start}
	visited[start] = true
	var nodes []int
	for len(stack) > 0 {
		u := stack[len(stack)-1] // pop the top of the stack
		stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]
		nodes = append(nodes, u)
		for _, v := range adj[u] {
			if !visited[v] {
				visited[v] = true // mark on push so no node is pushed twice
				stack = append(stack, v)
			}
		}
	}
	sort.Ints(nodes)
	return nodes
}

func main() {
	var n, m int
	fmt.Scan(&n, &m)
	adj := make([][]int, n)
	for i := 0; i < m; i++ {
		var u, v int
		fmt.Scan(&u, &v)
		adj[u] = append(adj[u], v)
		adj[v] = append(adj[v], u)
	}
	visited := make([]bool, n)
	count := 0
	for s := 0; s < n; s++ {
		if visited[s] {
			continue
		}
		count++
		fmt.Printf("component %d: %v\n", count, component(adj, visited, s))
	}
	fmt.Println("components:", count)
}

